{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "definition", "parent_tag": "00X0", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{D}$", "MATH_0003": "$f : \\mathcal{D} \\to \\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0004": "$u : \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{D}$", "MATH_0005": "$u_s$", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-morphism-sites}\n", "suffix": "\\end{definition}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:750c0231b84af082cca566c81778e9b14469921158fcfec20532b841502861bf", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:8947da48eb970f84dfe9148dd4b7e5b6c498708106b4ae6230706863050f377a", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> and <MATH_0002> be sites.\nA <TEXTITOPEN_0001>morphism of sites<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> <MATH_0003>\nis given by a continuous functor <MATH_0004>\nsuch that the functor <MATH_0005> is exact.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:104fb06481d4f661ffc4c229b881c515a67e360d61940935d4b1358f3349b723", "unit_id": "tag:00X1:statement"}
{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "00X0", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$u$", "MATH_0002": "$f$", "MATH_0003": "$f \\leftrightarrow u$", "MATH_0004": "$f^{-1} = u_s$", "MATH_0005": "$f_* = u^s$", "MATH_0006": "$f^{-1}$", "MATH_0007": "$f_*$", "MATH_0008": "$$\n\\Mor_{\\Sh(\\mathcal{D})}(f^{-1}\\mathcal{G}, \\mathcal{F})\n=\n\\Mor_{\\Sh(\\mathcal{C})}(\\mathcal{G}, f_*\\mathcal{F})\n$$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0002": "}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0003": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it ", "TEXTITOPEN_0002": "{\\it ", "TEXTITOPEN_0003": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:15160e827d2cc499cea5753d4b1fa8c2ee7cae732e2f78e35de5c90085b0116b", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:f8b38df9fb71e23a1d58a5dfa48cc7af2c9fb10ca0baa44b140a07d0c2f36255", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nNotice how the functor <MATH_0001> goes in the direction <TEXTITOPEN_0001>opposite<TEXTITCLOSE_0001>\nthe morphism <MATH_0002>. If <MATH_0003> is a morphism of sites\nthen we use the notation <MATH_0004> and <MATH_0005>.\nThe functor <MATH_0006> is called the <TEXTITOPEN_0002>pullback functor<TEXTITCLOSE_0002> and\nthe functor <MATH_0007> is called the <TEXTITOPEN_0003>pushforward functor<TEXTITCLOSE_0003>.\nAs in topology we have the following adjointness property\n<MATH_0008>\nThe motivation for this definition comes from the following\nexample.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:5b8153b39f61ec43fcc639624bac91196d8221422991181d2a0e2ae61254038f", "unit_id": "tag:00X0:prose-0001"}
{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "example", "parent_tag": "00X0", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$f : X  \\to Y$", "MATH_0002": "$X_{Zar}$", "MATH_0003": "$Y_{Zar}$", "MATH_0004": "$u : Y_{Zar} \\to X_{Zar}$", "MATH_0005": "$V \\mapsto f^{-1}(V)$", "MATH_0006": "$X_{Zar}$", "MATH_0007": "$Y_{Zar}$", "MATH_0008": "$u^s$", "MATH_0009": "$f_*$", "MATH_0010": "$u_s$", "MATH_0011": "$f^{-1}$", "MATH_0012": "$f^{-1} \\cong u_s$", "MATH_0013": "$f^{-1}$", "MATH_0014": "$u_s$", "MATH_0015": "$u$", "MATH_0016": "$f : X_{Zar} \\to Y_{Zar}$", "MATH_0017": "$f$", "MATH_0018": "$u_s, u^s$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{example-site-topological}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{remark-quasi-continuous}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{example}\n\\label{example-continuous-map}\n", "suffix": "\\end{example}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:7a92923b66de95fc86b43c2b211ea5a4949f6de6b636b63410d0f33013b4ca99", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:bc60f536898054ffd40ac55316e8ab96a25d909f2c4fcdc0b47c10d57b623af6", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a continuous map of topological spaces.\nRecall that we have sites <MATH_0002> and <MATH_0003>,\nsee Example <REF_0001>. Consider the functor\n<MATH_0004>, <MATH_0005>.\nThis functor is clearly continuous because inverse images of\nopen coverings are open coverings. (Actually, this depends on how\nyou chose sets of coverings for <MATH_0006> and <MATH_0007>.\nBut in any case the functor is quasi-continuous, see Remark\n<REF_0002>.)\nIt is easy to verify that\nthe functor <MATH_0008> equals the usual pushforward functor <MATH_0009>\nfrom topology. Hence, since <MATH_0010> is an adjoint and since\nthe usual topological pullback functor <MATH_0011> is an adjoint as well,\nwe get a canonical isomorphism <MATH_0012>. Since <MATH_0013>\nis exact we deduce that <MATH_0014> is exact. Hence <MATH_0015> defines a morphism\nof sites <MATH_0016>, which we may denote\n<MATH_0017> as well since we've already seen the functors <MATH_0018> agree\nwith their usual notions anyway.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:e269dd371a6a4705099c20580390f1d99f5d64996b26ddc58bd0b62e759e9f34", "unit_id": "tag:00X2:statement"}
{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "example", "parent_tag": "00X0", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0002": "$$\n\\text{Cov}(\\mathcal{C}) \\supset \\text{Cov}'(\\mathcal{C})\n$$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{C}_\\tau$", "MATH_0005": "$\\text{Cov}(\\mathcal{C})$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{C}_{\\tau'}$", "MATH_0007": "$\\text{Cov}'(\\mathcal{C})$", "MATH_0008": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0009": "$$\n\\epsilon : \\mathcal{C}_\\tau \\longrightarrow \\mathcal{C}_{\\tau'}\n$$", "MATH_0010": "$\\text{id} : \\mathcal{C}_{\\tau'} \\to \\mathcal{C}_\\tau$", "MATH_0011": "$\\tau'$", "MATH_0012": "$\\tau$", "MATH_0013": "$\\epsilon_* = \\text{id}^s$", "MATH_0014": "$\\epsilon^{-1}$", "MATH_0015": "$\\epsilon_*$", "MATH_0016": "$\\tau'$", "MATH_0017": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0018": "$\\tau$", "MATH_0019": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0020": "$\\tau'$", "MATH_0021": "$\\tau$", "MATH_0022": "$\\epsilon^{-1}$", "MATH_0023": "$\\epsilon^{-1}$", "MATH_0024": "$\\epsilon^{-1}$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-limit-sheaf}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{lemma-sheafification-exact}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{categories-lemma-exact-adjoint}", "REF_0004": "\\ref{definition-morphism-sites}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{example}\n\\label{example-finer-topology}\n", "suffix": "\\end{example}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:7bc2fe41b82f02fc4f4d7a3d2188a6bb2a55f590e81084937bc9e26367ed27c9", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:6617131154ec7742630da089ecf1b1447d6da46cbc06f465d49b5bf8ac945629", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a category. Let\n<MATH_0002>\nbe two sets of families of morphisms with fixed target\nwhich turn <MATH_0003> into a site. Denote <MATH_0004>\nthe site corresponding to <MATH_0005> and\n<MATH_0006> the site corresponding to <MATH_0007>.\nWe claim the identity functor on <MATH_0008> defines a morphism of sites\n<MATH_0009>\nNamely, observe that <MATH_0010>\nis continuous as every\n<MATH_0011>-covering is a <MATH_0012>-covering. Thus the functor\n<MATH_0013> is the identity functor on underlying\npresheaves. Hence the left adjoint <MATH_0014>\nof <MATH_0015> sends a <MATH_0016>-sheaf <MATH_0017>\nto the <MATH_0018>-sheafification of <MATH_0019> (by the\nuniversal property of sheafification).\nFinite limits of <MATH_0020>-sheaves agree with finite limits\nof presheaves (Lemma <REF_0001>) and <MATH_0021>-sheafification\ncommutes with finite limits (Lemma <REF_0002>).\nThus <MATH_0022> is left exact. Since <MATH_0023>\nis a left adjoint it is also right exact\n(Categories, Lemma <REF_0003>).\nThus <MATH_0024> is exact and we have checked all the\nconditions of Definition <REF_0004>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:9efea57a79456c7356500954a5756c97de68a2915333e739862b035434e5a0c4", "unit_id": "tag:0EWI:statement"}
